summary:
Dittmann and Pop prove that every finitely generated field K is characterized among finitely generated fields by one sentence in the language of rings (Theorem 1.1), and that every infinite one is bi-interpretable with ℤ (Theorem 1.2). In characteristic two and Kronecker dimension above three they assume resolution of singularities above F_2. The core is Theorem 1.3: for each d ≥ 3 the geometric prime divisors of finitely generated fields of Kronecker dimension d are uniformly first-order definable. The proof has four stages. First, anisotropic 'nice' Pfister forms are detected at prime divisors, through the cohomological local–global principles of Jannsen and Kerz–Saito and Gabber's prime-to-2 alterations (§§2–3). Then compactness of the patch topology isolates one divisor (Proposition 3.10, Theorem 4.2). Next, a definable finitely generated normal subring is built (§5). Finally the result of Aschenbrenner–Khélif–Naziazeno–Scanlon (AKNS) gives bi-interpretability. 154 items (29 library, 6 planned, 119 missing) are routed to a Part II of LogicAndDefinabilityInNumberTheory (finitely generated field definability), a Part II of Tau Ceti's QuadraticFormInvariants (higher Pfister forms), a Part II of AdicCoefficientsAndComparisons (prime-to-degree alterations) and four source routes. Seventeen mistakes are recorded. Among them:
- a false cohomological-dimension bound in Proposition 3.2(3);
- a missing bridge from AKNS Proposition 2.28, which concerns finitely generated structures, to finitely generated fields;
- an unargued first-order definition of niceness;
- the dimensions one and two of Theorem 1.2, which §5 does not treat.
Each has a repair.
